    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The economic theory behind the TPP posits that free trade increases overall global efficiency and consumer surplus, which should theoretically benefit the domestic working class through lower prices and more competition. However, neoclassical models often assume that capital and labor are highly flexible and that the gains from trade are shared equitably, an assumption critics argue is unrealistic in the short or medium term for vulnerable communities.\n\nIn reality, the \"political economy\" of trade is more complex because those who suffer job displacement (disproportionately the unskilled working class) lack the political power to force redistribution of the benefits that they see going to multinational corporations and highly skilled workers.\n\nWhile the agreement might have succeeded in creating long-term macroeconomic efficiency, it likely failed the specific test of \"cost-benefit\" analysis for the average American factory worker by exposing them to unfair competition without sufficient safety nets or local adjustment programs.",
